## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to derive nested-loop complexity where inner bound shrinks with i, revealing a harmonic-sum growth.

## Key Concepts
- Harmonic series: sum floor(n/i) ~ n log n
- Theta vs O, space O(1)
- Closed-form approximations vs exact sums

## Code Example
```javascript
function countPairs(n){
  let count=0;
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++){
    for(let j=1;j<=Math.floor(n/i);j++){
      count++;
    }
  }
  return count;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you prove the Theta(n log n) bound?
- How does changing inner bound to floor(n/i^2) affect complexity?
- What are practical implications for large n?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical sliding-window counting and maintaining top-k with bounded memory.

## Key Concepts
- Sliding window data structure
- Hashmap for counts
- Top-k extraction in bounded time
- Deterministic tie-breaking

## Code Example
````javascript
// Sketch
const W = 10000;
const windowQ = [];
const counts = new Map();
function applyBatch(batch){
  for (const x of batch){
    windowQ.push(x);
    counts.set(x, (counts.get(x) || 0) + 1);
    if (windowQ.length > W){
      const y = windowQ.shift();
      const ny = (counts.get(y) - 1);
      if (ny) counts.set(y, ny);
      else counts.delete(y);
    }
  }
  const top3 = [...counts.entries()].sort((a,b)=>b[1]-a[1]).slice(0,3).map(e=>e[0]);
  return top3;
}
````

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt to a dynamic window size?
- What optimizations exist to avoid full re-scan on every batch?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests incremental counting, batch updates, and tight per-event amortized bounds, plus modulo-based pair counting with constant extra space.

## Key Concepts
- Residue class bookkeeping with O(1) per update
- Pair counting with modulo constraints
- Amortized vs worst-case trade-offs

## Code Example
```javascript
// insert/update logic
function apply(x, D, freq, total, op){
  const r = x % D;
  const idx = (D - r) % D;
  if(op==='insert'){ total += freq[idx]; freq[r]++; }
  else { freq[r]--; total -= freq[idx]; }
  return total;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How does performance change as D scales with n?
- Can you adapt this to allow multiple modulo targets simultaneously?

## Why This Is Asked
New angle on complexity-analysis: maintaining LIS under online batched updates tests block-based design, amortized reasoning, and practical trade-offs beyond typical path/graph problems.

## Key Concepts
- Longest Increasing Subsequence (LIS) dynamics
- sqrt-decomposition / block partitioning
- per-block LIS profiles and cross-block composition
- amortized cost vs full recomputation
- practical optimizations and memory bounds

## Code Example
```javascript
// Simple block LIS recompute (tails-based)
function computeBlockLIS(block) {
  const tails = [];
  for (const x of block) {
    let i = 0, j = tails.length;
    while (i < j) {
      const m = (i + j) >> 1;
      if (tails[m] < x) i = m + 1; else j = m;
    }
    tails[i] = x;
  }
  return tails;
}

// Placeholder for merging block profiles into global LIS length
function mergeBlocks(blocks) {
  let tails = [];
  for (const b of blocks) {
    // apply b.profile to current tails (conceptual)
    tails = applyProfile(tails, b.profile);
  }
  return tails.length;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this for non-increasing or multi-criteria LIS (e.g., by value and position)?
- What would be the impact of choosing K adaptively based on B and n?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to reason about fixed-memory distinct counting in streaming data, a common real-world need for analytics in constrained environments.

## Key Concepts
- HyperLogLog for cardinality estimation
- Mergeable sketches across batches
- Relative error ~ 1.04/√m; memory fixed by m

## Code Example
```javascript
function hllAdd(registers, x, hash) {
  const m = registers.length;
  const i = hash & (m - 1);
  const w = hash >>> Math.log2(m);
  const rank = leadingZeros(w) + 1;
  registers[i] = Math.max(registers[i], rank);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you choose p in a live system with memory constraints?
- How would you explain the trade-offs of using HLL vs a linear-counting approach in presence of enormous M?
